## Motivation

`wan-ti2v` (Wan2.2-TI2V-5B, 704p/121f) was the largest remaining eager-vs-compile gap in the H100/H200 benchmark: eager 56.76 s vs compile 51.61 s denoise on H100 (−9.1%). A 5-step trace (99.5% GPU busy) shows why: the TI2V **per-token** adaLN path burns ~14% of every step in avoidable memory traffic —

- `(scale_shift_table.unsqueeze(0) + temb.float()).chunk(6, dim=2)` materializes the full `(B, S, 6, D)` activation in fp32 (a widening copy plus an add, ~8 GB round-trip per block at this shape): 623k µs of a 7.27M µs step;
- the six chunked slices are **strided**, so the `.contiguous()` calls inside the fused-norm wrappers (`ScaleResidualLayerNormScaleShift` et al.) copy each full `(B, S, D)` slice again: 427k µs of `direct_copy`;
- the flashinfer rope `cos_sin_cache` is re-concatenated per block although `freqs_cis` is fixed for the whole forward.

